window.FIXTURES=window.FIXTURES||{};window.FIXTURES["speech"]={"vid":"lnfE3bZs5gY","speaker":"David Ranibok Adeang","role":"President","country":"Naoero","region":"Asia-Pacific","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lnfE3bZs5gY","date":"20260922","title":"🇳🇷 Naoero - President Addresses United Nations General Debate, 81st Session | #UNGA","seconds":892,"words":2027,"counts":{"claims":24,"promises":1,"attacks":0},"topics":[{"topic":"Nauru name change","share":0.1502,"seconds":134},{"topic":"NATO","share":0.147,"seconds":131},{"topic":"Nauru ocean rights","share":0.0863,"seconds":77},{"topic":"Nauru vulnerability","share":0.0804,"seconds":72},{"topic":"Nauru diplomacy","share":0.0759,"seconds":68},{"topic":"Nauru identity","share":0.0746,"seconds":67},{"topic":"World Bank","share":0.0727,"seconds":65},{"topic":"Nauru economy","share":0.0716,"seconds":64},{"topic":"Nauru seabed mining","share":0.068,"seconds":61},{"topic":"Deep seabed mining","share":0.0673,"seconds":60},{"topic":"UNSC reform","share":0.0673,"seconds":60},{"topic":"Nauru recognition","share":0.0388,"seconds":35}],"timeline":[[1,69,"Nauru diplomacy"],[70,137,"Nauru identity"],[139,201,"Nauru name change"],[202,273,"Nauru name change"],[275,352,"Nauru ocean rights"],[353,413,"Nauru seabed mining"],[415,475,"Deep seabed mining"],[477,541,null],[543,606,"NATO"],[608,672,"Nauru economy"],[674,745,"Nauru vulnerability"],[747,812,"World Bank"],[814,881,"NATO"],[883,943,"UNSC reform"],[945,1008,null],[1009,1044,"Nauru recognition"]],"stances":{"Nauru diplomacy":"David Ranibok Adeang expresses greetings and states that his country's goal is to ensure no nation is left behind.","Nauru identity":"David Ranibok Adeang emphasizes the fundamental principle of equality for all peoples and introduces a personal reflection on Nauru's history and identity.","Nauru name change":"David Ranibok Adeang affirms that the country's people have always known their homeland by the name 'Na' and states that the recent restoration of the name 'Naoero' honors this history rather than erasing it.","Nauru ocean rights":"David Ranibok Adeang asserts that Nauru is a rightful participant in the future of the ocean and calls for global recognition of its identity, while emphasizing the legal obligations of the international seabed authority regarding deep seabed mining.","Nauru seabed mining":"David Ranibok Adeang states that Nauru has invested resources in deep seabed exploration, established a national regulator based on scientific evidence and advisory opinions, and consistently called for the completion of the mining code.","Deep seabed mining":"David Ranibok Adeang states that Nauru is waiting for legal clarity on deep seabed mining regulations after 15 years of compliance and notes the institution's internal division on the issue.","NATO":"David Ranibok Adeang states that his government is depositing instruments of withdrawal from NATO and establishing a trust fund to support the country's finances after the arrangement ends.","Nauru economy":"David Ranibok Adeang argues that Nauru's economic progress is not accurately reflected in current assessments due to an overreliance on bilateral revenue arrangements and geographic fragility.","Nauru vulnerability":"David Ranibok Adeang argues that current assessment methods based solely on income are insufficient for small island states and supports the adoption of a multi-dimensional vulnerability index to ensure fair evaluation and support.","World Bank":"David Ranibok Adeang urges the World Bank to consider vulnerability alongside income when determining eligibility for assistance.","UNSC reform":"David Ranibok Adeang argues that the UN Security Council is outdated and does not reflect the current geopolitical reality.","Nauru recognition":"David Ranibok Adeang challenges the assembly to honor its commitments by recognizing Nauru and treating it as a partner rather than leaving it in a state of vulnerability."},"claims":[{"t":92,"quote":"This is the last general debate of his tenure and my country records his gratitude for a decade in which the smaller states were heard more often than before.","topic":"Nauru identity","kind":"fact"},{"t":134,"quote":"For many years the world has known our country as Noru.","topic":"Nauru identity","kind":"fact"},{"t":139,"quote":"Noru is how our name sounded to those who found it hard to say and the world kept it. But it is not the name our people used among themselves.","topic":"Nauru name change","kind":"fact"},{"t":158,"quote":"Long before our country took its place among the family of nations, long before our name appeared on international records, our people knew our homeland by one name, Na.","topic":"Nauru name change","kind":"fact"},{"t":204,"quote":"my government's priority is investing in our people and our future generations.","topic":"Nauru name change","kind":"fact"},{"t":209,"quote":"Restoring our name gives back to them a piece of the inheritance because the identity handed down by our own ancestors has a place in the community of nations.","topic":"Nauru name change","kind":"fact"},{"t":296,"quote":"Mr. President, Narrow is an ocean state. We are not merely guardians of the ocean, but rightful participants in its future.","topic":"Nauru ocean rights","kind":"fact"},{"t":307,"quote":"For Narrow, the ocean represents both opportunity and responsibility. Never one without the other. Both are essential.","topic":"Nauru ocean rights","kind":"fact"},{"t":360,"quote":"For 15 years NA invested critical resources in sponsoring the exploration of a portion of the deep seabed.","topic":"Nauru seabed mining","kind":"fact"},{"t":370,"quote":"Our sponsored entity Nori on Nor ocean resources has contributed about one pabyte of data more than onethird of all claren kibbitanzone data held in the authorities's deep data.","topic":"Nauru seabed mining","kind":"number"},{"t":415,"quote":"And when progress stalled, we looked to the 1994 agreement and invoked the 2-year rule.","topic":"Deep seabed mining","kind":"fact"},{"t":426,"quote":"That was 5 years ago and today the rules remain unwritten.","topic":"Deep seabed mining","kind":"number"},{"t":430,"quote":"We are participating in the first contentious proceedings before the seabed disputes chamber because our obligations as a sponsoring state require legal clarity.","topic":"Deep seabed mining","kind":"fact"},{"t":442,"quote":"For 15 years we have done everything a member state can do consistent with our obligations as a sponsoring state and today we continue to wait.","topic":"Deep seabed mining","kind":"number"},{"t":564,"quote":"Before I depart New York, NATO will be depositing our instruments of withdrawal with the Rome Statute Repository","topic":"NATO","kind":"fact"},{"t":583,"quote":"we contribute more than 10% of our revenue every single year so that NATO has something to stand on when and if these arrangements end","topic":"NATO","kind":"number"},{"t":654,"quote":"In January 2027, NATO will be considered a high income country in the OECD system.","topic":"Nauru economy","kind":"fact"},{"t":668,"quote":"58% of our revenue comes from bilateral arrangements that are not ours to renew.","topic":"Nauru economy","kind":"number"},{"t":674,"quote":"87% of the food our people eat is purchased and imported. We have one runway, one port, one road that connects None of that appears in how we are assessed.","topic":"Nauru vulnerability","kind":"fact"},{"t":734,"quote":"As a country that is ranked the fifth most vulnerable country in the world on the index and the most vulnerable in the Pacific, we welcome these developments as positive steps.","topic":"Nauru vulnerability","kind":"fact"},{"t":761,"quote":"as the ADB already does","topic":"World Bank","kind":"fact"},{"t":863,"quote":"For 33 consecutive years, this assembly has called for an end to the embargo against NATO reiterates that call","topic":"NATO","kind":"fact"},{"t":932,"quote":"The security council does not reflect the world as it is. It reflects the world as it was in 1945 when most of the countries in this hall did not yet exist as sovereign states.","topic":"UNSC reform","kind":"fact"},{"t":1017,"quote":"Say our name. Let the waiting end.","topic":"Nauru recognition","kind":"fact"}],"promises":[{"t":564,"quote":"Before I depart New York, NATO will be depositing our instruments of withdrawal with the Rome Statute Repository","topic":"NATO","kind":"promise"}],"attacks":[],"greece":[],"said":[],"pickup":[],"transcript":[{"t":1,"topic":"Nauru diplomacy","text":"The assembly will now hear an address by his excellency David Ranibbuk Adyang, president of and head of state of the Republic of Nairo. I request protocol to escort his excellency and invite him to address the Mr. President, Mr. Secretary General, excellencies, distinguished delegates, I bring you warm greetings from my people and my country. We meet under the theme restoring trust, managing transformation. A United Nations that delivers for all. For my country, that means ensuring no nation, regardless of size or distance, is left behind as the world changes around us."},{"t":70,"topic":"Nauru identity","text":"Mr. President, let me begin by congratulating you on your election to preside over this 81st session. My delegation assures you of our full support and we thank your predecessor for the stewardship of the 80th session. We also pay tribute to Secretary General Antonio Gutz. This is the last general debate of his tenure and my country records his gratitude for a decade in which the smaller states were heard more often than before. I have traveled far to speak on matters that may seem distinct but are united by a single fundamental principle. The right of every people to be treated as an equal and to speak for itself. Mr. President, there are moments in the life of a nation when its people are called to remember who they have always This is such a moment for us. And so I begin with something deeply personal to my people and my country and that is our For many years the world has known our country as Noru."},{"t":139,"topic":"Nauru name change","text":"Noru is how our name sounded to those who found it hard to say and the world kept it. But it is not the name our people used among themselves. I rise on my first point not to announce a change but to affirm a truth. Long before our country took its place among the family of nations, long before our name appeared on international records, our people knew our homeland by one name, Na. That name carries the memory of our ancestors, our language, and our identity. It has been there all along, never lost, never absent. Today, we restore NATO. We do not make this choice to erase our past. We make it because we honor it. We do so with respect for our history and with gratitude for the journey. NorU has carried us through to our independence and our place on the international stage."},{"t":202,"topic":"Nauru name change","text":"Mr. President, my government's priority is investing in our people and our future generations. Restoring our name gives back to them a piece of the inheritance because the identity handed down by our own ancestors has a place in the community of nations. Because their history and language matter because they too have the right to inherit a nation that knows itself, names itself and speaks of itself. In light of this year's theme, let me say this. There can be no genuine trust and no true transformation without respect for the identity, history, language and rights of every people to be treated as an equal and to speak for I take the opportunity now to convey my sincerest gratitude to the secretariat and to this assembly and the many institutions and partners who have taken steps to recognize our restored name. It may seem a small courtesy but it means a great deal at home. And so, Mr. President, I say this with the greatest That means I am from NATO and so is every person I have the honor to speak for today."},{"t":275,"topic":"Nauru ocean rights","text":"We take our seats under that name. Proud of where we have come from. Confident in who we are and determined to carry our name into the future. What I ask of the world costs nothing. Join us in recognizing and respecting the identity that is already ours. Say our name. Mr. President, Narrow is an ocean state. We are not merely guardians of the ocean, but rightful participants in its future. For Narrow, the ocean represents both opportunity and responsibility. Never one without the other. Both are essential. or the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea is left hollow. Today I focus on a part of it that belongs to no one, the deep seabed area beyond national jurisdiction. Mr. President, the convention established the international seabed authority and charged it with a clear legal obligation to put in place regulations and regulate activities in parallel with sponsoring Its role is not to determine whether deep sea bed mining is as a matter of policy public policy a correct course of action."},{"t":353,"topic":"Nauru seabed mining","text":"NA joined trusting in the commitment set out in the convention and the 1994 agreement. For 15 years NA invested critical resources in sponsoring the exploration of a portion of the deep seabed. Our sponsored entity Nori on Nor ocean resources has contributed about one pabyte of data more than onethird of all claren kibbitanzone data held in the authorities's deep data. We called on the authority to seek an advisory opinion on the responsibilities of sponsoring states and we followed it. We established a national regulator with the powers and due diligence obligations that opinion requires. At every stage, our decisions have rested on the best available scientific evidence on the convention and on that opinion. At every stage, we have called for the mining code to be finished."},{"t":415,"topic":"Deep seabed mining","text":"And when progress stalled, we looked to the 1994 agreement and invoked the 2-year rule. We were told then that we were rushing. That was 5 years ago and today the rules remain unwritten. We are participating in the first contentious proceedings before the seabed disputes chamber because our obligations as a sponsoring state require legal clarity. For 15 years we have done everything a member state can do consistent with our obligations as a sponsoring state and today we continue to wait. Mr. President, there's a general understanding within the authorities council that nothing is agreed until everything is agreed and that the regulations will be adopted by Achieving that is a great challenge for an institution whose members remain deeply divided on whether deep seabed mining should proceed at all."},{"t":477,"topic":null,"text":"a question the convention never asked the authority to decide. NATO welcomes every effort to ensure that seabed mineral development proceeds And I ask this assembly to reckon with this. A principle declared in 1970 and a bargain struck in 1994. Both in this hall still wait for the rules that would give them effect. When a multilateral process cannot deliver, the world does not stand still. Those with the means to act will not wait. And every year of delay makes that more likely not less. That is why the theme of this session matters. A multilateral system that cannot deliver for a state like mine does not deliver at all. That holds here in this assembly and in any institution we build. NATO therefore calls upon the authority. Let the waiting end adopt the rules."},{"t":543,"topic":"NATO","text":"Similarly, Mr. President, a multilateral agency with which we see of increasing to the NATO, namely the International Criminal Court is one which calls for NATO to end its participation and to revoke its membership. Before I depart New York, NATO will be depositing our instruments of withdrawal with the Rome Statute Repository. Mr. President, my government's central purpose is to put NATO on its own feet. That work is underway. Our trust fund to which we contribute more than 10% of our revenue every single year so that NATO has something to stand on when and if these arrangements end. Our fisheries, the seabed joining the Eggmons group. New revenue through our investment and residence programs. A virtual asset regulator built with full due diligence obligations before the industry regulates."},{"t":608,"topic":"Nauru economy","text":"Our valuable, our valuable partnerships, and behind all of it, investment in health, education, and sport so that our people are not only better off, but healthier and better prepared. We are building an economy that not that does not depend on any single arrangement. And by every measure of fiscal discipline, we have done what was asked of us. Yet the measure by which we are assessed sees none of it. It sees income and it sees nothing else. It does not capture the full story of my nation, the true cost of our geographic the fragility of our limited economic base, or the devastating impact a single disaster can have on our people and our progress. In January 2027, NATO will be considered a high income country in the OECD system. That does reflect real progress. But progress should not be punished because 58% of our revenue comes from bilateral arrangements that are not ours to renew."},{"t":674,"topic":"Nauru vulnerability","text":"87% of the food our people eat is purchased and imported. We have one runway, one port, one road that connects None of that appears in how we are assessed. That is why we strongly support the multi-dimensional vulnerability index or MVI and the United Nations efforts to pilot the tool. Its full implementation is essential to ensuring that countries like ours are fairly assessed and adequately supported. It requires it recognizes that vulnerability is complex and it must be seen, measured and acted upon. Mr. President, we acknowledge the significant process progress made by some development partners and financial institutions in adopting vulnerability assessments and reviewing the eligibility criteria. The Asian Development Bank ADB weighs vulnerability alongside income and its own policy names my nation as the clearest example of why income alone conceals what small island states face. As a country that is ranked the fifth most vulnerable country in the world on the index and the most vulnerable in the Pacific, we welcome these developments as positive steps."},{"t":747,"topic":"World Bank","text":"But the gap remains. Not all have taken the necessary steps to truly recognize our realities. I therefore urge all who have yet to do so, including the World Bank, to weigh vulnerability alongside income in decisions on eligibility as the ADB already does. If the international community is truly serious about restoring trust and managing it must demonstrate that commitment where it matters most with those who are most vulnerable. Mr. President, I turn to my final point. No country of our size stands alone. NATO is a friend to all and an enemy to NATO's partnerships are not all of one Some are born of kinship, some of shared interests. Some have been built through decades of cooperation, and some are grounded in the conviction that when a wrong persists, it must be named and Each relationship matters. None is a substitute for another and none comes at the expense of another."},{"t":814,"topic":"NATO","text":"Mr. Present small states are increasingly asked to choose between partners, between blocks, between competing visions of the world. NATO would not be made to choose. We will work with every partner who respects our sovereignty and our right to decide our own future. That is not neutrality. It is called independence. Partnership does not mean agreement on everything. It means standing together where interests align and standing on principle where they do not. A friend I speak for today has been waiting for justice. I am asking this assembly to honor its word and I cannot then fall silent when that word is broken for someone else. For 33 consecutive years, this assembly has called for an end to the embargo against NATO reiterates that call and we encourage dialogue grounded in full respect for sovereignty because sovereign equality must be more than a principle written in the charter."},{"t":883,"topic":"UNSC reform","text":"Whatever this assembly intends to deliver, it will be delivered through partnership and cooperation or not at So I extend an invitation to the members of this assembly. Invest in our people. Invest in our economy that rests on more than a handful of arrangements. Invest in institutions that will outlast my government and the next. Invest in the infrastructure, connectivity and opportunities that distance has too often denied us. What NATO offers this assembly is not dependence but Not expectation but contribution. Not a not a beneficiary but a partner. Invest with us not simply in us. Mr. president I've raised today concerns this organization and what it has promised. So does this. The security council does not reflect the world as it is. It reflects the world as it was in 1945 when most of the countries in this hall did not yet exist as sovereign states."},{"t":945,"topic":null,"text":"NATO therefore calls for a reform that expands both the permanent and non-permanent categories. A body that speaks for all of us should look like all of us. Mr. President, as I draw my statement to a close, the matters I have raised may appear separate. They are not. Our name is about sovereignty. How we are measured is about justice. The deep seabed is about opportunity and Our partnerships are about standing as One principle binds them. the right of every people to be treated as an equal and to speak for itself. You have convened this assembly to restore trust, to manage transformation and to deliver for all. Trust is restored when promises are kept. Transformation is managed when decisions lead to action. And we deliver for all only when you deliver for the small. Everything NATO has asked for today was agreed in this hall long before I took this floor."},{"t":1009,"topic":"Nauru recognition","text":"I am not asking this assembly to be braver than it has ever been. I am challenging you to be as good as your word. Say our name. Let the waiting end. Let vulnerability be measured honestly and treat us as partners. May God bless the Republic of NATO. May God bless the United Nations. Mate, I thank you. On behalf of the assembly, I wish to thank the president and head of state of the republic of Nairo."}],"compare":[{"topic":"NATO","share":0.147,"mean":0.0011,"rank":1,"others":4},{"topic":"Nauru","share":0.0759,"mean":0.0,"rank":1,"others":0},{"topic":"Global Economy","share":0.0727,"mean":0.0142,"rank":16,"others":31},{"topic":"Ocean Governance","share":0.0673,"mean":0.0064,"rank":9,"others":16},{"topic":"UN & its reform","share":0.0673,"mean":0.2055,"rank":161,"others":170}]};
